#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139522} :activity/id #uuid "4e2e5333-ed8e-576e-abed-94cc93efbdb5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051 :topic/id #uuid "d5e55293-c14f-55db-a945-4aa2b42df552" :topic/math-academy-id 2310} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139662 :tutorial/content "A **compound inequality** is an inequality that consists of multiple different inequalities put together. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\nx \\leq 0 \\hspace{.25cm} \\text{ or } \\hspace{.25cm} x \\gt  3\n$$\n\n states that $x$ is less than or equal to $0$, or greater than $3$.\n\nFor the compound \"or\" inequality to be satisfied, at least one of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-1$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x \\leq 0.$\n\n- $x=5$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x > 3.$\n\n- $x=1$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x \\leq 0$ and it also does not satisfy $x > 3.$\n\nTo graph a compound inequality involving the word \"or,\" we graph the two separate inequalities on the same number line. In this particular case,\n\n- the inequality $x \\leq 0$ consists of a closed circle at $0$ and an arrow to the left, while\n\n- the inequality $x >3$ consists of an open circle at $3$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/96/96f1b1ee25ebc210a83ba12d90396683b7e2660050f820c4057ab85de419db91.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "770f70a7-6710-533c-bd9a-e37123e913bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8132 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "73ffc612-4a54-5b2a-9949-3131ab4b5010" :step/math-academy-id 79780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138073 :question/id #uuid "014723fc-0b7d-5212-a41e-73847f5adb00" :question/math-academy-id "e-13011"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6090342d-3d4e-519f-ab7d-3fb6df6e92a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050519}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447251}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing \"Or\" Inequalities on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "1d905dee-c60b-587c-8b1e-96a234f4883a" :step/math-academy-id 79781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138537 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138075 :question/id #uuid "e5b6a9dc-3a19-5496-9e22-d953ff4eb647" :question/math-academy-id "e-13018"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32daa2fb-e5fa-5e44-b6f2-5d815d9145af" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447252}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying \"Or\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "bcf79e23-3398-56f2-9f7c-6dddc11d8b32" :step/math-academy-id 79784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139663 :tutorial/content "Compound inequalities can also be written with the word \"and.\" However, such inequalities are often written together as a single expression with two inequality signs. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x < -1\n$$\n\n means that $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\nFor the compound \"and\" inequality to be satisfied, both of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-3$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies both $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\n- $x=-5$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $-4 \\leq x.$\n\n- $x=0$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x < -1.$\n\nTo graph this compound inequality, we first graph the two inequalities separately.\n\n![](images/42/42edf8ee61badbbd1e5525a3843cfbe189e6432b2ad06455c1bdca6a7087ac56.png)\n\nThen, we keep only where they overlap.\n\n![](images/fa/fa07233f96bed30ddfcc5905a55a835763359cd34188e4d1c249c13b1d27d25c.png)\n\n**Note:** Any compound \"and\" inequality will always represent a segment between two points. So, it is not always necessary to draw both inequalities and find the overlap. To speed up the process, we can just draw the endpoints with open or closed circles as appropriate, and highlight the area between the endpoints." :tutorial/id #uuid "9859e4f2-2267-558c-923f-35c348957ad9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8133 :tutorial/title "Compound \"And\" Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "59dbf0dd-b3a9-5867-8cbf-3b28624f9ad9" :step/math-academy-id 79788 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138538 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138074 :question/id #uuid "21eace75-0b5a-5b37-903c-c238e3821bee" :question/math-academy-id "e-13012"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9106723c-b8b0-586e-8c2b-9bb4f50bc945" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447250}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing \"And\" Inequalities on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "7e38370d-1c18-5d27-bb22-cf9444dd1b90" :step/math-academy-id 79789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138539 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138076 :question/id #uuid "347520ff-e759-5d99-acf7-0bc8a8f26689" :question/math-academy-id "e-13019"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a707a741-0208-5a74-8478-ac05ff2673ab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447249}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying \"And\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines"} :step/id 